  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5b627d61",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### CdA and Crr \n",
    "#### Definition \n",
    "CDA and Crr are two main parameters of the bike simulation. \n",
    "The Crr is the Coefficient rolling resistance. It depends essentially on \n",
    "- the nature of the road and the type of surface (more or less smooth or rough asphalt, concrete, gravel, etc.)\n",
    "- the tires used (size, pressure, material)\n",
    "\n",
    "The aerodynamic drag coefficient (CdA) represents the air resistance of a cyclist in motion. It depends on multiple parameters, including:\n",
    "- The rider's position on the bike\n",
    "- The shape and design of the bike and its components\n",
    "- The cyclist's body type\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Simple table\n",
    "The following table could be useful for a quick start : \n",
    "\n",
    "| Activity | Cda range| Crr range| Mean [CDA - CRR]|\n",
    "| ---- | --- | --- | --- | \n",
    "|*Bikepacking-Travel*  |0.45-0.65 |0.005-0.007 | **[0.55 - 0.006]**|\n",
    "|*Gravel bike*| 0.35-0.55|0.005-0.007 |**[0.40 - 0.006]**|\n",
    "|*Amateur road cycling* |0.30-0.40|0.004-0.005 |**[0.35  - 0.0045]**|\n",
    "|*Pro Road cycling*| 0.24-0.32 |0.003-0.004 |**[0.28  - 0.0035]**|\n",
    "|*Pro Triathlon-Time trial*| 0.17-0.24 |0.003-0.004|**[0.21 - 0.0035]**|\n",
    "\n",
    "For more precise settings, you can:\n",
    "- For Crr visit the website \"https://www.bicyclerollingresistance.com/\". They provide Crr values for many tires (adjust according to load and pressure).\n",
    "- For CdA, calibrate it using one or more simulations (see above).\n",
    "\n",
    "The proposed simulation is primarily designed for a cyclist riding alone. \n",
    "\n",
    "Riding in a group or peloton will significantly reduce the impact of wind by decreasing aerodynamic drag, and this effect will vary for each rider depending on their position within the group. \n",
    "If you try to use it on group rides, one possible solution is to reduce the coefficient of aerodynamic drag (CdA) you would have when riding solo. \n",
    "You could also try calibrating the CdA based on real-world rides under the same conditions. \n",
    "\n",
    "😆 Perhaps in the future, I will offer a reduction coefficient adapted to different riding styles within a group (e.g., \"hero,\" \"supportive,\" \"opportunistic,\" \"wheel drafting,\" etc.).\n",
    "   \n",
    "### Mass M\n",
    "The Mass N corresponds to the combined weight of the bicycle and the cyclist (in clothing). It must be indicated in kg.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The wind (grib)\n",
    "At any time, for each segment of the course, the simulation needs to determine the wind value based on position and time. This is done by interpolating the values ​​from the GRIB files (standardized data formatused in meteorology to store historical and forecast weather data) in space and time. \n",
    "So you have to give the grib object created previously by the weather module. \n",
    "\n",
    "But you can also make a simulation with no wind. In this case, the GRIB parameter will not be specified or will be set to None.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The route\n",
    "\n",
    "The simulation is applied to a given route using a GPX file. A raw GPX file should preferably be pre-processed beforehand to provide the simulation methods with more suitable data.\n"
   ]
  },
